e right y-axis displays the size measurements of the LNP obtained using DLS\n\nFigure caption: In vitro validation of the LNP properties following the size control. (a) Luciferase expression and cell viability in HeLa cells at 6 h post-transfection of luciferase-encoding mRNA LNPs. (N = 6 biological replicates) (b) Western blot analysis of HeLa cells at 6 h post-transfection of luciferase-encoding mRNA LNPs represented in β-actin normalized bar graph. (c) Representative fluorescence microscopic images of HeLa cells treated with GFP-encoding mRNA LNPs at 12 h post-transfection. (Green = GFP, Blue = Nuclei) (d) Surface ionization levels of LNPs measured by the TNS binding assay to obtain pKa values. (N = 3 technical replicates) (e) Generalized polarization (GP) values of LNPs from the Laurdan hydration assay for measurement of membrane fluidity. (N = 3 technical replicates) (f) Hemolytic activity of LNPs measured under different pH conditions. (N = 3 technical replicates) (g) Number of particles formulated within fixed excipients ratio and volume measured by nanoparticle tracking analysis. (h) Representative fluorescence histogram of HeLa cells into which DiD-loaded LNPs were delivered upon incubation in endocytosis inhibitors for 20 min (Ncell = 10,000 per condition)\n\nFigure caption: Cellular uptake of LNPs with different sizes in HeLa and modeling. (a) Representative fluorescence microscopic images of DiD-loaded LNP-treated HeLa cells at various time points. Scale bar = 200 μm. (b) Cellular uptake shown as mean fluorescence intensities of HeLa cells treated with DiD-loaded LNPs at different time points. (N = 3 biological replicates) (c) Illustration of active particle endocytosis showing the effects of wrapping angle (θ). The upper panel (Small LNP) represents conditions where (θ/π >0.35) favoring successful uptake, while the lower panel (Large LNP) indicate (θ/π >0.35), resulting in ineffective membrane interaction. Using the theoretical model \\documentclass[12p